espace-pre-wrap break-words">Total duration: 35 minutes
I feel like this was the worst interview experience this year. When I first=
 made the appointment, there was no email, only a phone call to schedule th=
e interview time. When I got to the interview, I found out that I had to in=
stall DingTalk =F0=9F=98=93... The company's network downloads were slow, a=
nd after I finally downloaded it, I found out that I hadn't logged into my =
account for a long time and needed to scan the code with DingTalk on my pho=
ne, but I don't have DingTalk on my phone =F0=9F=98=93... So they said I ha=
d to use my phone for the interview, and then my phone battery was low, so =
I asked the interviewer if I could use WeChat. The interviewer replied that=
 WeChat couldn't be installed on Alibaba computers =F0=9F=98=93... In the e=
nd, I had no choice but to find a charger to charge my phone, and when the =
interview started, I found that 15 minutes had passed...
The interview experience wasn't very good either. I had no idea what the in=
terviewer was asking or what kind of person they were looking for. I though=
t I answered the technical questions well, but the complexity of the projec=
t didn't quite meet the interviewer's expectations. Ultimately, this was th=
e fastest interview I've had this year.
Introduce a more complex architectural project in your recent work experien=
ce
I'm a little unsure how to answer this question, because my recent work exp=
erience has mainly been focused on internal innovation. I've tried many dif=
ferent directions and projects, and during development, I've tried to base =
secondary development on open source projects as much as possible, so there=
 aren't any projects that are particularly complex in terms of architecture=
.
At the beginning, I introduced the relatively complex functional points in =
the project, but the interviewer said that this was not what he wanted, so =
he introduced a project with a relatively complex architecture, but it was =
based on the open source project.
Talk about the implementation of Promise
Talked about several key technical points of Promise implementation:
1. The key is to implement <code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 b=
order-border-300 text-danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 =
py-px text-[0.9rem]">Promise.then</code>the method
2. Maintain an <code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border=
-300 text-danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[=
0.9rem]">fullfilled</code>event queue and an <code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 b=
order border-0.5 border-border-300 text-danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap roun=
ded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">rejected</code>event queue
3. In <code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text=
-danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">=
Promise.then</code>the method, you need to determine the current Promise st=
atus and parameter type
4. Finally, we need to implement the self-execution of two event queues to =
handle chain calls
5. <code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-da=
nger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">set=
Timeout</code>Use mock async tasks when executing methods
Why use setTimeout?
The main purpose is to simulate the asynchronous execution of Promise, whic=
h is better used in NodeJS <code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 b=
order-border-300 text-danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 =
py-px text-[0.9rem]">process.nextTick</code>, because the asynchronous exec=
ution of Promise belongs to microtasks, while setTimeout is actually a macr=
otask, which is not accurate enough. In addition, the event loop mechanism =
of the browser is discussed:
1. The JS thread will execute the code synchronously. When encountering an =
asynchronous task, it will notify other threads of the browser (such as the=
 timer thread) to execute the asynchronous task and put the callback of the=
 asynchronous task into an event queue.
2. When the synchronous code is executed, the callback task in the asynchro=
nous event queue will be executed
3. Tasks in the asynchronous event queue can be divided into macrotasks and=
 microtasks. Macrotasks mainly refer to asynchronous tasks that rely on ext=
ernal threads, while microtasks do not rely on external threads.
4. When executing asynchronous event queues, microtasks are always executed=
 first, that is, if there are microtasks in the queue, then the microtasks =
are executed, otherwise the macrotasks are executed.
5. When the asynchronous event queue is empty, an event loop ends
